1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,575
The Singapore Stone: The Inscribed
Rock Singapore Blew Up in 1843

2
00:00:05,575 --> 00:00:11,315
In January 1843, on the orders of the man
in charge of Singapore's public works,

3
00:00:11,395 --> 00:00:14,880
a great slab of red
sandstone at the mouth of the

4
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:20,125
Singapore River was blown apart to
clear the ground for Fort Fullerton.

5
00:00:20,125 --> 00:00:24,799
The slab had stood on the point since
long before the British arrived,

6
00:00:24,879 --> 00:00:28,750
and its face carried about
fifty lines of carved writing.

7
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:31,841
It was the only ancient
inscription ever found

8
00:00:31,921 --> 00:00:34,805
on the island, and in
more than two hundred

9
00:00:34,885 --> 00:00:38,125
years of trying, nobody
has managed to read it.

10
00:00:38,125 --> 00:00:43,498
In June 1819, a few months after
Stamford Raffles founded the settlement,

11
00:00:43,578 --> 00:00:46,487
workers clearing the jungle at the point

12
00:00:46,567 --> 00:00:50,750
on the south side of the river
mouth uncovered the slab.

13
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:55,014
According to Dr Montgomerie, one
of the colony's first surgeons,

14
00:00:55,094 --> 00:00:58,340
the men who found it were
Bengali sailors working

15
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:02,425
for Captain Flint, the settlement's
first Master Attendant.

16
00:01:02,425 --> 00:01:06,913
They were frightened by the writing
and refused to go on clearing,

17
00:01:06,993 --> 00:01:09,900
and Chinese workers had to finish the job.

18
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:14,350
The stone was about three metres
high and three metres wide.

19
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:17,882
It was not a single block
but half of a great boulder

20
00:01:17,962 --> 00:01:21,494
that had been split in two,
and the two halves leaned

21
00:01:21,574 --> 00:01:25,050
towards each other at an
angle of about 40 degrees.

22
00:01:25,050 --> 00:01:29,655
The writing was cut into the
flat inner face of one half:

23
00:01:29,735 --> 00:01:32,696
some fifty lines inside a raised rim,

24
00:01:32,776 --> 00:01:37,625
covering an area about 1.5
metres high and 2.1 metres wide.

25
00:01:37,625 --> 00:01:41,467
The first careful description
came from John Crawfurd,

26
00:01:41,547 --> 00:01:47,206
who stopped in Singapore on his way to
Siam as the Governor-General's envoy and

27
00:01:47,286 --> 00:01:52,225
examined the stone early on the
morning of the 4th of February 1822.

28
00:01:52,225 --> 00:01:56,230
He found the carving far rougher
than anything he had seen in

29
00:01:56,310 --> 00:02:00,517
Java or India, and the writing
so worn, more by the decay of the

30
00:02:00,597 --> 00:02:04,950
rock than by age, that it was
"quite illegible as a composition".

31
00:02:04,950 --> 00:02:08,300
Here and there a few
letters still stood out.

32
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:10,579
He guessed that the script was Pali,

33
00:02:10,659 --> 00:02:13,150
the religious script of the Buddhists.

34
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:17,097
Munshi Abdullah, the Malay
scribe whose memoir is one of the

35
00:02:17,177 --> 00:02:21,124
few local accounts of these
years, remembered that people of

36
00:02:21,204 --> 00:02:25,500
every community crowded round the
stone as soon as it was found.

37
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:28,182
The Hindus said the writing was Hindu

38
00:02:28,262 --> 00:02:30,950
and the Chinese said it was Chinese.

39
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:34,783
Abdullah went with Raffles and
the missionary Claudius Thomsen,

40
00:02:34,863 --> 00:02:37,515
and from the shape of
the letters he thought

41
00:02:37,595 --> 00:02:40,825
it might be Arabic, but he
could not read it either.

42
00:02:40,825 --> 00:02:45,218
Visitors pressed flour and lard into
the grooves and lifted them out,

43
00:02:45,298 --> 00:02:47,746
hoping to get the shape of the letters,

44
00:02:47,826 --> 00:02:51,975
and others poured a black liquid
over the face, without success.

45
00:02:51,975 --> 00:02:56,109
Raffles himself, according to
an account published in 1834,

46
00:02:56,189 --> 00:03:00,609
tried strong acids to bring out
the characters, and that failed

47
00:03:00,689 --> 00:03:04,975
too. (The Munshi Abdullah post
tells the rest of his story.)

48
00:03:04,975 --> 00:03:08,141
The Sejarah Melayu, the
court chronicle of the

49
00:03:08,221 --> 00:03:11,528
Malay kings of Singapura
and Melaka, has a story

50
00:03:11,608 --> 00:03:14,925
that many people have
connected with the stone.

51
00:03:14,925 --> 00:03:19,125
Badang was a slave who
cleared forest for his master.

52
00:03:19,125 --> 00:03:23,375
One day he caught a spirit in his
fishing net, and in return for

53
00:03:23,455 --> 00:03:27,650
its freedom the spirit made him
the strongest man in the land.

54
00:03:27,650 --> 00:03:32,500
The king of Singapura brought him
to court and made him a commander.

55
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:36,580
When a king in India sent his
own champion to challenge him,

56
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:41,375
the contest was to lift a huge rock
that lay in front of the palace.

57
00:03:41,375 --> 00:03:45,521
The Indian champion lifted it
and dropped it, but Badang lifted

58
00:03:45,601 --> 00:03:49,425
it and threw it towards the
mouth of the Singapore River.

59
00:03:49,425 --> 00:03:53,367
From the 1830s, writers on
Singapore linked the inscribed

60
00:03:53,447 --> 00:03:57,460
slab with this story, either
as the rock that Badang threw

61
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:01,350
or as one of the stones said
to have marked his grave.

62
00:04:01,350 --> 00:04:04,533
Nothing beyond the legend
ties the two together,

63
00:04:04,613 --> 00:04:09,100
and the stone itself has never
been read well enough to settle it.

64
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:13,496
In November 1842, Captain
D. H. Stevenson took over

65
00:04:13,576 --> 00:04:17,700
as Superintendent of
Convicts and Public Works.

66
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:20,646
Two months later he
ordered the stone blasted,

67
00:04:20,726 --> 00:04:25,645
because the point was needed for the
commander's quarters at Fort Fullerton,

68
00:04:25,725 --> 00:04:28,225
the fort that guarded the river mouth.

69
00:04:28,225 --> 00:04:32,454
Antiquarians protested, among
them Lieutenant-Colonel James

70
00:04:32,534 --> 00:04:36,550
Low of the East India Company,
but the work went ahead.

71
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:40,200
The blame was often laid on someone else.

72
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:44,050
The 1921 history One Hundred
Years of Singapore called

73
00:04:44,130 --> 00:04:49,436
the man responsible "a vandal at the
head of the Public Works Department",

74
00:04:49,516 --> 00:04:53,002
and some accounts named
George Drumgoole Coleman,

75
00:04:53,082 --> 00:04:55,775
the architect who had held that post.

76
00:04:55,775 --> 00:04:59,166
But Coleman had left for Europe in 1841,

77
00:04:59,246 --> 00:05:01,850
and the order was Stevenson's.

78
00:05:01,850 --> 00:05:05,241
After the explosion, Low
picked out the fragments

79
00:05:05,321 --> 00:05:08,003
with the most legible writing and had a

80
00:05:08,083 --> 00:05:11,625
Chinese mason chisel them
down into smaller slabs.

81
00:05:11,625 --> 00:05:16,525
He gave one to the Governor, Samuel
George Bonham, and kept three.

82
00:05:16,525 --> 00:05:20,689
Bonham's piece did not stay safe
for long: it ended up as a seat

83
00:05:20,769 --> 00:05:24,137
for the guards at the
Treasury, until his successor,

84
00:05:24,217 --> 00:05:28,925
William John Butterworth, recognised
it and took it into his own house.

85
00:05:28,925 --> 00:05:31,600
Other pieces fared worse.

86
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:35,361
One merchant later recalled
a large fragment lying at the

87
00:05:35,441 --> 00:05:39,943
corner of Government House on
Government Hill, today's Fort Canning,

88
00:05:40,023 --> 00:05:43,325
which convict labourers
broke up to build a road.

89
00:05:43,325 --> 00:05:47,050
Abdullah's verdict on the
whole affair was blunt.

90
00:05:47,050 --> 00:05:50,311
He thought it was done from
"ignorance or stupidity",

91
00:05:50,391 --> 00:05:54,345
that now "we can never know the
nature of this ancient writing",

92
00:05:54,425 --> 00:05:58,775
and he ended with a Malay saying:
"What you can't make, don't break."

93
00:05:58,775 --> 00:06:03,764
In 1848 J. W. Laidlay of the
Asiatic Society of Bengal asked

94
00:06:03,844 --> 00:06:08,575
for the fragments, so that
the Society could study them.

95
00:06:08,575 --> 00:06:13,207
Butterworth's piece left Singapore
on the 19th of February 1848

96
00:06:13,287 --> 00:06:17,700
aboard the barque Rob Roy, and
Low sent his three after it.

97
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:21,308
By July all four were in
Calcutta, where Laidlay swept

98
00:06:21,388 --> 00:06:24,859
fine charcoal dust into the
worn grooves to make the

99
00:06:24,939 --> 00:06:28,900
letters stand out and drew
them for the Society's journal.

100
00:06:28,900 --> 00:06:33,050
The fragments later passed
to the Calcutta museum.

101
00:06:33,050 --> 00:06:36,050
They stayed there for seventy years.

102
00:06:36,050 --> 00:06:41,475
In 1918 the Raffles Museum in
Singapore asked for them back.

103
00:06:41,475 --> 00:06:47,278
Calcutta returned one fragment, on
indefinite loan, and it arrived in 1919,

104
00:06:47,358 --> 00:06:51,750
the year Singapore marked the
centenary of its founding.

105
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:54,875
What happened to the others is not known.

106
00:06:54,875 --> 00:06:57,864
The scholars who have
tried to read the stone

107
00:06:57,944 --> 00:07:01,150
have not even agreed on
what language it is in.

108
00:07:01,150 --> 00:07:06,263
Bland, who made the 1837 copy,
called the script "ancient Ceylonese,

109
00:07:06,343 --> 00:07:10,311
or Pali", and James Prinsep,
the Calcutta scholar who

110
00:07:10,391 --> 00:07:15,050
deciphered the ancient Brahmi
script of India, partly agreed.

111
00:07:15,050 --> 00:07:18,190
Laidlay, working on the fragments in 1848,

112
00:07:18,270 --> 00:07:21,563
thought it was Kawi,
the old script of Java,

113
00:07:21,643 --> 00:07:25,400
and suggested it might
record a Javanese victory.

114
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:30,725
Later Dutch and Indonesian scholars
each came to a different answer.

115
00:07:30,725 --> 00:07:33,602
J. H. C. Kern made out a few words

116
00:07:33,682 --> 00:07:36,900
and dated the writing to around 1230.

117
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:41,373
N. J. Krom thought it looked
like the script of Majapahit,

118
00:07:41,453 --> 00:07:44,750
the Javanese empire, but from before 1360.

119
00:07:44,750 --> 00:07:48,182
J. G. de Casparis suggested the 10th or

120
00:07:48,262 --> 00:07:51,775
11th century and possibly Old Javanese.

121
00:07:51,775 --> 00:07:56,966
Boechari placed it no later than the
12th century, with links to Sumatra,

122
00:07:57,046 --> 00:08:01,450
and suggested the language was
Sanskrit rather than Javanese.

123
00:08:01,450 --> 00:08:04,982
John Miksic, the archaeologist
best known for his work

124
00:08:05,062 --> 00:08:08,595
on Fort Canning, has argued
that the person who had it

125
00:08:08,675 --> 00:08:11,672
carved was more likely
Sumatran than Javanese,

126
00:08:11,752 --> 00:08:16,100
and that the inscription will
probably never be fully deciphered.

127
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:20,940
The National Museum dates it to
between the 10th and 14th centuries

128
00:08:21,020 --> 00:08:25,500
and describes it as written in
Kawi with some Sanskrit words.

129
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:28,975
The newest readings
keep the question open.

130
00:08:28,975 --> 00:08:34,150
In 2019 the researcher Iain Sinclair
reported a sequence of letters,

131
00:08:34,230 --> 00:08:39,095
"kesariva", that he suggested
could be part of Parakesarivarman,

132
00:08:39,175 --> 00:08:44,275
a title used by several kings of
the Chola dynasty of south India.

133
00:08:44,275 --> 00:08:49,175
If that is right, the stone could
date from the early 11th century.

134
00:08:49,175 --> 00:08:52,725
It remains one researcher's proposal.

135
00:08:52,725 --> 00:08:57,679
In 2023 the linguists I-Shiang Lee
and Francesco Perono Cacciafoco

136
00:08:57,759 --> 00:09:03,476
compared the stone's letters with Kawi
writing and found close similarities,

137
00:09:03,556 --> 00:09:07,675
but also differences in how
the vowel marks were used.

138
00:09:07,675 --> 00:09:12,772
The piece that came back from
Calcutta is about 74 centimetres wide,

139
00:09:12,852 --> 00:09:16,125
a small part of the
slab that Crawfurd saw.

140
00:09:16,125 --> 00:09:21,108
It is the only fragment in
Singapore, and in 2006 the National

141
00:09:21,188 --> 00:09:26,250
Museum of Singapore named it one
of its 11 National Treasures.

142
00:09:26,250 --> 00:09:30,257
It is on show in the museum's
Singapore History Gallery,

143
00:09:30,337 --> 00:09:35,300
where it is described as the oldest
writing ever found in Singapore.

144
00:09:35,300 --> 00:09:40,225
The stone also turns up in the
country's public celebrations.

145
00:09:40,225 --> 00:09:44,625
The 2016 National Day Parade
told the story of Badang and

146
00:09:44,705 --> 00:09:48,950
showed him lifting a glowing
stone covered in letters.

147
00:09:48,950 --> 00:09:53,036
And in 1972 the Merlion statue
was unveiled at the mouth

148
00:09:53,116 --> 00:09:57,053
of the river, a short distance
from where the slab had

149
00:09:57,133 --> 00:10:01,075
stood. (The Merlion post
covers that statue's story.)

150
00:10:01,075 --> 00:10:05,800
Fort Fullerton, the fort the stone
was cleared for, has gone too.

151
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:09,805
Its site is now the Fullerton
Building, opened in 1928

152
00:10:09,885 --> 00:10:13,587
as the General Post Office
and today the Fullerton

153
00:10:13,667 --> 00:10:17,600
Hotel (the Fullerton Building
post tells its story).

154
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:22,100
Nothing on the waterfront marks
the place where the stone stood.

155
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:27,176
In 1843 there was no law in
Singapore to protect an old monument,

156
00:10:27,256 --> 00:10:30,825
and the stone stood in
the way of a building.

157
00:10:30,825 --> 00:10:35,234
That changed in the early
1970s, when the Preservation of

158
00:10:35,314 --> 00:10:39,566
Monuments Act set up a board
to protect historic sites,

159
00:10:39,646 --> 00:10:44,450
and the first eight national
monuments were gazetted in 1973.

160
00:10:44,450 --> 00:10:48,595
Today the National Heritage
Board's Preservation of Sites

161
00:10:48,675 --> 00:10:52,825
and Monuments division looks
after more than 70 of them.

162
00:10:52,825 --> 00:10:56,383
The Singapore Stone came
too early for any of that:

163
00:10:56,463 --> 00:10:59,735
what survives is one
fragment in a museum case,

164
00:10:59,815 --> 00:11:03,025
a set of drawings and
a copy made with dough.

165
00:11:03,025 --> 00:11:06,774
Why it matters today:
Singapore's written history

166
00:11:06,854 --> 00:11:10,369
is usually said to begin
with Raffles in 1819,

167
00:11:10,449 --> 00:11:14,825
and its older past with a few
lines in the Malay Annals.

168
00:11:14,825 --> 00:11:18,307
The Singapore Stone is the
one piece of writing from

169
00:11:18,387 --> 00:11:22,006
that older past that was
actually found on the island,

170
00:11:22,086 --> 00:11:25,100
carved centuries before
the British arrived.

171
00:11:25,100 --> 00:11:28,383
Most of it was lost in a morning in 1843,

172
00:11:28,463 --> 00:11:32,975
and the fragment that survives
still has not been read.

173
00:11:32,975 --> 00:11:36,650
It is a reminder that the
record of early Singapore is

174
00:11:36,730 --> 00:11:40,543
thinner than it needs to be,
partly because of decisions

175
00:11:40,623 --> 00:11:44,100
made by the people who
were building the new town.
